<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|notes>

<\body>

  <chapter*|A living scientific record>

  <notes-abstract|The journal system was built to circulate and certify
  papers. It is strained by its own incentives, and now by machines that
  produce plausible text at almost no cost. This essay argues for
  recentering scholarly communication on the sharing of meaning: a record
  in which original results, reviews, comments, comparisons and
  distillations remain connected, and in which journals are one way among
  several of selecting and consolidating them. Its guiding values are
  attention and care: noticing what matters, and taking responsibility for
  it. In mathematics especially, the important work is moving from
  establishing facts to distilling them into concepts that can be
  understood and shared.>

  <section*|A system under strain><label|sec:crisis>

  Scientific journals were created to circulate results and to keep a
  record of who found what. Over time they have come to carry much more:
  editorial judgment, peer review, selection, preservation, and the
  prestige on which careers and funding depend. These functions require
  people and money, and any alternative has to keep them going. The trouble is that all of them hang on a single event, the
  publication of a paper. Making work public, reviewing it, recommending it
  and assigning credit become hard to tell apart, and where a result
  appeared starts to stand in for what it establishes. <hlink|DORA's
  declaration|https://sfdora.org/read/> is one well-known objection to this
  substitution.

  The strain was visible before the recent advances in artificial
  intelligence: more papers than anyone can read, reviewers asked too
  often, corrections that reach only some of the readers of the original.
  Language models change the scale of the problem. A plausible paper, a
  plausible referee report or a plausible reply can now be produced at
  almost no cost, while checking a reference, finding a hidden assumption
  or understanding what a result is good for takes as long as it ever did.
  A system that counts papers and citations rewards the cheap part. The
  exchange of article, report and reply can then run its course without
  anyone checking that the words match the work.

  The question is not whether a text was written with machine assistance.
  A model can help produce a useful conjecture, a translation or a checked
  proof; human writing can be empty. Disclosure helps us interpret a
  contribution, but cannot measure the attention it received. What matters
  is what someone has answered for. The record should show who examined
  what, and on what evidence.

  <section*|Recentering on meaning><label|sec:meaning>

  The crisis is also an occasion to ask what the scientific record is for.
  By <em|record> I mean the scholarly record in the usual sense; the
  question is less what it stores than what it lets us carry on.
  A result matters through what it allows others to understand: the
  problems it resolves, the examples that illuminate it, the questions it
  leaves open, the way it changes how a field sees itself. Science is a
  collective work of communication, of sharing meaning between people who
  do not see the same things, across disciplines and generations. What it
  produces is less a pile of papers than an intellectual heritage, coherent
  enough to be learned and built upon, and varied enough to hold different
  points of view.

  I find it helpful to think of this heritage as a web of stories, an
  image I owe to Tim Ingold. The
  same theorem appears in the paper that proved it, in the textbook that
  made it standard, in the lecture that explained why it is true, in the
  survey that placed it beside its competitors, and in the counterexample
  that showed where it stops. Each tells it differently, for a different
  reader, and none of them is the theorem itself. Understanding a subject means being able to move between these
  stories. A record that keeps only the first of them, the original paper,
  loses much of what makes the result usable.

  <section*|Mathematics as distillation><label|sec:distillation>

  In mathematics this is especially clear, and the name already says
  so. The Greek <em|mathemata> are the things that can be
  learned: what passes from one person to another in a form both can
  understand. Mathematics in this original sense is a conceptual
  patrimony, which exists only in being shared intelligibly. Yet much of its
  system of credit rewards establishing facts: proving that a statement
  holds, computing a constant, classifying the cases. Such facts now accumulate
  faster than anyone can absorb them, and machines will produce many more:
  computations, formal proofs, conjectures tested on millions of examples.
  A raw mathematical fact, however certain, is not yet understanding. The
  work that increasingly matters is distillation: letting results sediment
  and mature, finding the idea that makes them natural, and extracting what
  Rabelais called the <em|substantifique moelle>, the marrow inside the
  bone.

  Distilling does not mean simplifying. It shapes an idea in precise and
  airtight language, so that it can be checked, and at the same time in a
  form that can be understood and passed on. Precision and clarity are
  often treated as competing demands; in a good definition or a good proof
  they turn out to be the same thing, and that is what lets a result be
  shared.

  In a world that grows more complex, and in which more of its description
  is produced by machines, understanding cannot remain the private
  achievement of a few specialists, and neither can responsibility for what
  is claimed. A record of mathematics should
  therefore make room for the slow work through which facts become
  concepts, and recognize it as research.

  <section*|Many kinds of contribution><label|sec:kinds>

  To hold this web of stories, the record has to make room for the
  different contributions it is made of:

  <\itemize>
    <item><em|original contributions>: new results, methods, constructions
    and data;

    <item><em|reviews>: assessments by someone who has examined a
    contribution, with the scope of what they checked;

    <item><em|comments>: objections, questions, requests for clarification,
    remarks that a step does not follow, accounts of approaches that
    failed;

    <item><em|comparisons>: several results, methods or proofs placed side by
    side, showing what each achieves and where they differ;

    <item><em|rereadings>: returns to an older work with new questions or new
    tools, which can change what it is taken to show;

    <item><em|distillations and expositions>: accounts that rework a result
    or a body of results until its central ideas are clear, often shorter and simpler than any of the
    sources.
  </itemize>

  Only the first kind is fully recognized today. The others exist, in
  confidential referee reports, in correspondence, in lecture notes, in
  blog posts and in the introductions of later papers, but they are
  scattered, hard to find and rarely credited. Yet a good distillation can
  do more for a field than several new results, and a careful rereading can
  change what a classic is understood to prove. Nor is the list fixed:
  inquiry often changes the categories it starts with, and the record
  should let its own categories change too.

  <section*|Attention and care><label|sec:attention>

  Keeping these contributions together takes more than storage. The
  record should help people reconstruct an argument, see its limits, find
  connections and carry an inquiry further. Two words name what this asks
  of us.

  I take <em|attention> to mean the work of noticing what matters: an
  unstated hypothesis, an unexpected observation, a question concealed by
  familiar terminology. <em|Care> is the responsibility that lets this
  attention have consequences: checking the reference, maintaining the
  data, answering the objection, or developing an explanation another
  person can use. Both require time and conditions in which people can
  participate, including newcomers and critics in precarious positions.

  That is why distillations, rereadings and explanations belong in the
  record: an explanation can change what a reader is able to see, and even
  a failed approach can clarify the question.

  Three authors have shaped how I think about this. None of them wrote
  about scholarly infrastructure; the application is mine.

  <subsection*|Ingold: learning to attend><label|sec:ingold>

  Tim Ingold's <hlink|education of
  attention|https://lchc.ucsd.edu/mca/Paper/ingold/ingold1.htm>, a phrase he
  takes from James J. Gibson, describes learning as guided engagement
  rather than the transfer of content. A student can possess every paper in a field and still
  struggle to recognize its important questions. Working through a proof
  with someone who knows where the difficulty lies can change what the
  student notices on the next reading.

  Ingold's later account of <hlink|correspondence|https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9655.12541>
  emphasizes attention and response in activities that develop together.
  I read this as an invitation to keep the ways into a problem: worked examples, reading paths, questions and explanations of
  failed approaches. An annotation cannot replace apprenticeship, but it
  can let another reader pick up where the encounter left off.

  In <em|Being Alive>, Ingold contrasts knowledge integrated along the
  paths of stories with knowledge assembled by fitting particulars into
  classes. The web of stories in which a theorem lives is meant in this
  sense: its versions are not instances of one master text, but lines along
  which understanding has traveled and can travel again.

  <subsection*|Girard: the limits of transparency><label|sec:girard>

  In <hlink|<em|Le fantôme de la transparence>|https://girard.perso.math.cnrs.fr/transparence.pdf>,
  Jean-Yves Girard criticizes the fantasy of a completely explicit order
  whose discovery would remove the need to keep asking questions. I see a
  related temptation in scholarly infrastructure: to suppose that encoding,
  linking and ranking all results would make their significance evident.

  Formal verification is a good example. Checking a proof can
  establish a conclusion within a specified formal environment. We still
  have to ask whether the formalization captures the intended problem,
  whether its assumptions are useful, and what the result helps us
  understand. A proof can pass the checker while the paper announces a
  more general theorem than the one checked; someone still has to compare
  the two.

  <subsection*|Lolli: the movement of an argument><label|sec:lolli>

  Gabriele Lolli begins <em|Matematica come narrazione> (in English,
  <em|The Meaning of Proofs>) by proposing that a
  proof can be constructed and understood as a narrative. His concern is
  mathematical content, rather than the lives of mathematicians or a
  summary of established results (see the <hlink|excerpts from the
  preface|https://www.tecalibri.info/L/LOLLI_narrazione.htm>).

  Consider the proof that there are infinitely many primes. Multiply any
  finite list of primes and add one: the result has a prime divisor outside
  the list. Understanding the strategy means seeing how the proposed list
  supplies the means of escaping it. An exposition makes that movement
  visible and helps the reader recognize it elsewhere. A dependency list
  alone may leave the decisive idea obscure; a narrative keeps it, and
  different narratives can make it visible to different readers.

  <section*|A glimpse of a possible structure><label|sec:structure>

  To review something, we must know exactly what it is. A paper is one
  piece of work, but not the only one: a piece of code, a dataset, a
  formal proof, a set of lecture notes or a single lemma with its proof can
  each be examined, used and cited on its own, and so can a review, a
  comment or a distillation. If each is identified exactly, version by
  version, refereeing becomes a statement about a particular piece of
  work, made by a particular person or community with a stated scope,
  rather than a property acquired once and for all by appearing in a
  journal.

  A journal then appears as a selection: a community's reasoned choice of
  the work it considers worth reading, together with the reviews that
  justify it. Separated from the deposit of the work, selection can be
  practiced by a journal, a society, a seminar or a single teacher, each
  explaining its criteria and standing behind its choices. Different
  communities can select the same work differently. Selection is also the
  first step of consolidation. Over time, results are gathered into
  narratives of how a problem came to be understood, into corpora for a
  field, and into theories that make a body of knowledge coherent. This is
  how scattered results become an understanding of the world, physical and
  mathematical. That work, too, should be identifiable and open to review
  and revision.

  A result then lives as a small web: the version that was refereed and
  the reports on it, the objection that found a gap and the correction it
  prompted, the later proof that went further, the comparison of the two,
  the notes that made it teachable. Today a reader of the original has no
  reliable way to find them. The aim is to keep them
  connected, each identified exactly and each attributable to someone who
  answers for it.

  This website is a small experiment in keeping a work and its discussion
  together. Each post of the series on
  <notes-link|observables|self-adjoint-operators.tm>
  has a companion with the proofs, a comment section, and dated notes when
  something is corrected; the sources of all pages are public, with their
  full history. What such a personal arrangement cannot do is keep these
  pieces connected beyond one author's care: the objection in a comment,
  the correction note and the version it corrected all live on one host.

  I would like to explore a scientific record in which these contributions
  stay together and can be carried from one institution or platform to
  another. Its technical core can be surprisingly simple.

  <section*|A simple technical shape><label|sec:implementation>

  Researchers should never have to see a hash. Authoring
  tools can offer familiar actions, such as publishing a version,
  responding to a review or correcting a passage, and do the rest
  underneath. What lies underneath is built from well-tested pieces.

  The first is the cryptographic hash. A hash function such as SHA-256
  turns any file into a short fingerprint; changing a single byte changes
  the fingerprint completely, and finding two files with the same
  fingerprint is practically impossible. Using the fingerprint as the
  identifier gives a link that cannot silently point to different content.
  Anyone can recompute it, any copy found anywhere can be checked against
  it, and no central registry is needed to issue it.

  The second is the log, as in Git, the version control system that most
  software and many mathematical projects already use. Each version of a
  work is a commit: a small record naming, by their hashes, the files it
  contains and the commit or commits it continues, together with an author,
  a date and a message. Since a commit's hash covers its parents, the hash
  of the latest commit fixes the whole history before it, and a change to
  the past cannot go unnoticed. Releases,
  approvals and corrections can be recorded as further entries, and signed.

  Everything else can be stored as small structured documents in open
  formats such as JSON or YAML, each following a published schema: the
  metadata of a work, a review with its scope, an annotation naming a
  passage, a status notice, a selection with its entries. The contributions
  themselves stay in their own open formats: TeX, TeXmacs, Markdown, Lean,
  CSV.
  Such files can be read by people and by programs, compared line by line,
  copied with ordinary tools and still understood when today's services
  are gone.

  Parts of this already exist. Software Heritage archives public source
  code under content-derived identifiers, standardized in 2025 as
  <hlink|ISO/IEC 18670|https://www.iso.org/standard/89985.html>; arXiv keeps
  every version of a preprint; <hlink|PubPeer|https://pubpeer.com/> collects
  comments on published papers; a Lean
  <hlink|blueprint|https://github.com/patrickmassot/leanblueprint> links an
  informal argument to its formal counterpart. Each solves part of the
  problem within one service. What is missing is a way for the pieces to
  survive together.

  Hashes and logs settle integrity and history, and nothing more. They say
  nothing about who is entitled to release a version, a log kept by one
  host does not keep the files available, and a work cannot point to the
  reviews written after it. Signatures, independent copies and indexes that
  collect later contributions are still needed, and so are the institutions
  that run them. The point of a simple backbone is that these services can
  change, or disappear, while the record remains checkable. Nothing here
  requires a token or a global blockchain: objects can travel through
  ordinary web services, offline exports or networks such as
  <hlink|IPFS|https://docs.ipfs.tech/concepts/persistence/>.

  Such a record should start small: one scholarly community, two
  independently operated repositories, and the history of a few results,
  together with their reviews and corrections, kept usable after one
  repository disappears. It should be judged by whether participants
  understand more, not by how much it holds or how fast it moves.

  <section*|What technique cannot provide><label|sec:limits>

  Being checkable does not make a record trustworthy, available or
  understood, and much of the remaining work consists in keeping these questions
  apart.

  A signature shows that a statement was made with a certain key.
  Connecting the key to a person, and the person to the authority to
  release a version, needs further evidence, and none of it establishes
  expertise or truth. A widely mirrored, correctly signed paper can be
  wrong; an important historical manuscript can have no digital signature
  at all. Assessments, in turn, need scope: \PI checked Proposition 2\Q and
  \PI recommend this introduction\Q say different things, and a critic's
  objection does not carry the authority of an editor's withdrawal.
  Compressing all this into a single \Pverified\Q badge would be
  Girard's phantom of transparency again, and would recreate the substitution of
  venue for content.

  Nor does any of this make truth depend on approval: a counterexample can
  defeat a claim endorsed by an entire community. The record should
  preserve the reasons for a judgment and the opportunity to challenge it.
  The coherence of a story, institutional prestige and agreement cannot replace
  evidence or proof.

  Keeping files is not preserving a record. Someone must collect what is
  written later, keep copies, check them and hand them on when an
  institution closes. Preservation remains an institutional commitment,
  and it should protect work that is rarely read today: present popularity
  tells us little about what another generation will need.

  <strong|Attention is the scarce resource.> Open participation cannot promise
  unlimited reviewing time, least of all when generated submissions and
  replies cost almost nothing. Counting endorsements is no remedy, since
  endorsements can be automated, traded or coordinated. Newcomers need
  routes to attention that do not depend on insider sponsorship, and the
  editing, preservation and teaching on which all this rests need to be
  paid and recognized. A numerical \Pcare score\Q would merely create
  another activity to optimize.

  <section*|Continuing the inquiry><label|sec:continuing>

  Stable references, attributable judgments and portable collections can
  help us return to an argument and continue it. They cannot supply the
  understanding that makes the return worthwhile. However much of the text
  is produced by machines, that remains the work of people who notice a
  difficulty, develop an explanation and <strong|take responsibility> for what they
  pass on. In this the record is like mathematics itself, made of
  <em|mathemata>, things learned from one another, where understanding
  something and answering for it go together.

  This is the sense in which I want the scientific record to be alive.

  <section*|Reading notes><label|sec:reading-notes>

  Most of the texts below are available online.

  <\itemize>
    <item>François Rabelais, <em|Gargantua>, prologue: the reader is
    invited to \Prompre l'os et sucer la substantifique moelle\Q of the
    book (modernized spelling).

    <item>Tim Ingold, <em|From the Transmission of Representations to the
    Education of Attention>, in <em|The Debated Mind> (2001), and
    <em|On Human Correspondence>, <em|JRAI> 23(1) (2017), 9\U27.
    <em|Being Alive> (2011), in particular the chapter \PStories against
    classification\Q, and <em|The Life of Lines> (2015) develop related
    images of paths, relations and their continuation; the web of stories
    of this essay draws on them.

    <item>Jean-Yves Girard, <em|Le fantôme de la transparence>, essay
    dated 6 September 2010, later expanded in the
    <hlink|2016 book|https://girard.perso.math.cnrs.fr/Transparence.html> of
    the same title (Allia). Girard presents the book in two radio interviews
    published by Éditions Allia, for
    <hlink|France Culture|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fFEHUcRFDHc> and
    <hlink|Radio Campus Orléans|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D-hxSy7BFBk>.
    His CIRM lecture <hlink|<em|Deux ou trois choses que je sais d'elle : la
    logique>|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KoPkIChIs54> (2022) gives his
    broader view of logic, and his Roma Tre seminar
    <hlink|<em|La logique déconfinée>|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k1caYYWsfy4>
    (2020) a more technical account of the same views.

    <item>Gabriele Lolli, <em|Matematica come narrazione> (il Mulino,
    2018), <em|Premessa>, p. 7 onward; English translation <em|The Meaning
    of Proofs: Mathematics as Storytelling> (MIT Press, 2022). A detailed
    review by Miglena Asenova and Bruno D'Amore appeared in <em|La matematica
    e la sua didattica> 26(2) (2018), 311\U316
    (<hlink|PDF|https://site.unibo.it/rsddm-dm/it/pubblicazioni/recensione-960-asenova-d-amore-recensione-a-lolli-2018-1.pdf/@@download/file/Recensione-960-Asenova-D-Amore-Recensione-a-Lolli-2018%5B1%5D.pdf>).
    His later article <hlink|<em|Matematica e
    letteratura>|https://doi.org/10.33683/ddm.24.16.2>, <em|Didattica della
    matematica> 16 (2024), 36\U60 (open access under CC BY 4.0;
    <hlink|PDF|a-living-scientific-record-assets/lolli-matematica-e-letteratura.pdf>,
    also on
    <hlink|ResearchGate|https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386105200_Matematica_e_letteratura>),
    uses the example of Italo Calvino to argue that mathematics is
    narrative and that narrative can follow paths opened by mathematics,
    and draws consequences for teaching. The prime-number example above is
    my illustration of the connection.
  </itemize>
